course: medicine
lesson: 16
title: A classmate with diabetes, and other long-term conditions
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Your body runs on sugar from food, and a hormone (a chemical messenger in the blood) called insulin is the key that lets the sugar into your cells.
explain: In type 1 diabetes the immune system has destroyed the insulin-making cells in the pancreas, so the child gives insulin by injection or pump and checks blood sugar with a finger prick or a sensor.
explain: It is not caused by eating sweets and you cannot catch it; with careful carb counting a diabetic child can even eat the birthday cake.
explain: Too little sugar is a hypo: shaky, pale, sweaty, cross or confused; fast sugar like juice or glucose tablets fixes it.
explain: Many classmates live with long-term conditions, epilepsy, cystic fibrosis, ADHD, heart problems, and none of them is catching or anyone's fault.
fact: In type 1 diabetes the immune system has destroyed the insulin-making cells of the pancreas, an organ behind the stomach; insulin lets sugar from food into cells for energy.
fact: Type 1 diabetes is not caused by eating too many sweets, and it is not catching.
fact: A continuous glucose monitor has a tiny thread under the skin that measures sugar every few minutes and sends it to a phone.
fact: A hypo is blood sugar too low, from too much insulin, too little food or extra exercise; signs are shaking, sweating, paleness, crossness or confusion.
fact: A hypo is treated with fast sugar such as glucose tablets or juice, then the level is checked again in 15 minutes.
fact: Type 2 diabetes is commoner in older adults; the body makes insulin but doesn't respond well, and it is treated with diet, exercise, tablets and sometimes insulin.
fact: Children with type 1 diabetes can eat most foods if they count the carbohydrates (carbs, the sugar and starch in food) and adjust their insulin.
fact: Epilepsy causes seizures from bursts of electrical activity in the brain; nothing should be put in the mouth, and nobody can swallow their tongue.
fact: During a seizure, adults keep the person safe by moving hard things away, time it, put them on their side afterwards, and call 999 if it lasts over five minutes or is a first seizure.
fact: A wheelchair is a tool that gives freedom; ask before pushing someone's wheelchair, and schools plan trips so that everyone can come.
fact: Cystic fibrosis is an inherited condition (passed down from parents) with thick sticky mucus (slime) in the lungs and gut, treated with medicines and daily chest physio; it is not catching, but two people with it keep apart so they do not pass lung germs to each other.
fact: ADHD is a difference in how the brain manages attention, activity and impulses (sudden urges), and it is not caused by bad parenting or sugar.
fact: Some children are born with heart conditions, and heart doctors called cardiologists decide which sports are safe.
exercise: What does insulin do? | Lets sugar from food get into cells | Makes food taste sweeter on the tongue | Turns all the sugar you eat into fat | Stops you feeling hungry between meals
exercise: What causes type 1 diabetes? | The immune system destroying insulin-making cells | Eating far too many sweets and fizzy drinks as a child | Catching it from a friend | Not drinking enough water
exercise: What does the little sensor on your friend's arm do? | Measures his sugar level every few minutes | Plays music into his headphones | Gives him insulin all by itself through the skin | Counts his steps and nothing else
exercise: Your diabetic classmate goes shaky and pale. What's likely? | A hypo, blood sugar too low | He's being naughty to get out of work | He's got a cold coming on | He needs more insulin straight away to calm down
exercise: What fixes a hypo? | Fast sugar, like glucose tablets or juice | A big glass of cold water and a long sit down | A nap on the beanbag | Another dose of insulin
exercise: How is grandad's type 2 diabetes different? | His body makes insulin but doesn't respond well | He caught it from eating one big cake at a party long ago | It is exactly the same as type 1 | He has no pancreas at all
exercise: Can a child with type 1 diabetes eat birthday cake? | Yes, if he counts the carbs and adjusts his insulin | No, never any sugar, not even on birthdays | Yes, as much as he likes, without any care | Only the icing
exercise: What should you never do during someone's seizure? | Put anything in their mouth | Move hard things away from them | Time how long the seizure lasts | Go and get an adult straight away
exercise: When should 999 be called for a seizure? | If it lasts over five minutes or is a first one | Every time, after just one second | Never, because seizures always stop on their own in time | Only if the person is a grown-up
exercise: What should you do before pushing someone's wheelchair? | Ask them first | Just push it fast to help | Take the brakes off quietly | Tell a teacher to push it
exercise: Why does a friend with cystic fibrosis have physio every day? | To clear thick sticky mucus from the lungs | Because it's fun and he likes it | To build big strong arm muscles | To stop it spreading to the others in his class at school
exercise: What is ADHD? | A difference in how the brain manages attention and activity | Being naughty on purpose | Eating too much sugar at breakfast | Bad parenting at home
exercise: Who decides what sport is safe for a child with a heart condition? | A cardiologist, a heart doctor | The PE teacher, on his own, after a lesson | His friends in the football team | Nobody, he can never run at all
exercise: Can you catch diabetes? | No | Yes, by sharing drinks | Yes, by touching | Only in hospital
exercise: Can a person swallow their tongue during a seizure? | No, they can't | Yes, always, so watch out | Yes, so you must hold it | Only children can do that
exercise: What can cause a hypo? | Too much insulin, too little food or extra exercise | Too much sleep at the weekend and not enough water in the week | Too little screen time | Drinking lots of water
test: kid in my class has diabetes, mate says it's 'cos he ate too many sweets. true? | No, type 1 is the immune system destroying insulin-making cells | Yeah, too many sweets wear out the bit that makes insulin | Yeah, and chocolate makes it worse | No, it's from catching a bad cold
test: Tom is cross and confused before lunch, and his sensor beeps "low". What should happen? | An adult gives him fast sugar and checks again in 15 minutes | He gets a detention for being rude | He has a bit more insulin | He runs around the field a few times to wake himself up
test: In a film, someone shoves a spoon in a person's mouth during a seizure. What's wrong? | Nothing should go in the mouth; they can't swallow their tongue | Nothing, that's right | A fork would be better | The spoon should be a soft wooden one so it doesn't break their teeth
test: Why might a diabetic child need a snack before a big football match? | Exercise uses sugar and could cause a hypo | Football makes you diabetic | Snacks cure his diabetes for the whole of an afternoon | So he can run faster than all the others
test: Mia uses a wheelchair. Why can she come on every school trip? | Schools plan trips so everyone can come | She can't, wheelchairs aren't allowed on trips | She has to stay behind at school | Only if she walks part of the way
test: Josh with ADHD takes medicine at lunch. Jake says his mum spoilt him. Right? | No, ADHD isn't caused by bad parenting | Yes, being spoilt at home is the real cause of it | Yes, too much sugar did it | No, he caught it from watching TV
test: Two people with cystic fibrosis are told to keep apart. Why, if it's not catching? | To avoid passing germs between them | Because they don't like each other | Because CF does spread by touch after all | Because it's a rule for fun
test: Ben has a heart condition and sits out some PE. Someone calls him lazy. Fair? | No, his heart doctor decides what is safe | Yes, he's just lazy | Yes, PE fixes hearts, so he should join in | No, he's just shy
test: Your grandad and your friend both have diabetes, but only your friend has injections. Why? | Grandad has type 2, which is often treated with tablets and diet | Grandad is braver and doesn't need them | Your friend is faking | Old people can't have injections
test: What would happen if a type 1 diabetic body got no insulin at all? | Sugar couldn't get into the cells for energy | Nothing, insulin isn't really needed | He'd become stronger | He'd simply turn into a type 2 diabetic instead
test: A girl in class falls and shakes in the hall. What should the children do? | Get an adult and move hard things away | Hold her still tightly until it stops | Put a pencil between her teeth | Pour cold water on her face
test: My friend can eat cake, so he can't really be diabetic. Smart? | No, with carb counting and insulin he can eat most foods | Yes, diabetics can't eat cake | Yes, he's lying | No, cake cures diabetes
exam: Identify the function of the hormone insulin. | It enables glucose derived from food to enter the cells | It renders food sweeter to the taste | It suppresses all sensation of hunger between the principal meals | It converts water into energy
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises type 1 diabetes? | A condition in which the insulin-producing cells have been destroyed by the immune system | A condition caused by the excessive consumption of confectionery | A contagious condition transmitted by touch | A condition identical to type 2 diabetes
exam: It is given that a pupil with diabetes exhibits tremor, pallor and confusion. State the appropriate immediate measure. | The administration of rapidly absorbed sugar | The administration of a further dose of insulin | The imposition of strenuous exercise | The withholding of all food and drink
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning first aid during an epileptic seizure. | Nothing is to be placed in the mouth of the person | A rigid object is to be placed between the teeth | The person is to be restrained forcibly | The tongue is to be grasped to prevent its being swallowed
